    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Almost every driveway sealing article assumes you're starting from bare, unsealed asphalt or concrete. Most driveways aren't — they're being resealed, and treating that like a first-time job is exactly how you end up with unnecessary two-coat applications, or worse, sealer piled on top of sealer until it cracks off in sheets.
        </p>

        <div id="first-time-vs-reseal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Resealing vs. First-Time Sealing: Why the Difference Matters</h2>
          <p>
            First-time sealing is applied to a bare, cured surface with no coating history — it needs full prep, crack filling, and typically two coats for even coverage and color. Resealing is applied over an existing, previously-sealed surface, and the whole calculus changes: the surface already has a base layer of protection, so the job is about assessing that layer's condition, cleaning it properly, and adding the right amount of fresh product — not repeating a full first-time application from scratch.
          </p>
          <p>
            Get this wrong in either direction and you create a problem. Under-treating a driveway that's actually stripped bare (like after aggressive pressure washing removed all the old sealer) leaves it under-protected. Over-treating a driveway that's still in good shape — full two-coat applications every year regardless of condition — is the single most common cause of the cracked, flaking sealer buildup covered further down this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-1" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Step 1: Confirm You Actually Need to Reseal</h2>
          <p>Run two simple tests before buying anything:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The color test:</strong> Has the surface faded from deep black to gray or brown-gray? That's a visible sign the UV protection in the existing coat has worn through.</li>
            <li><strong>The water-bead test:</strong> Pour a cup of water on the driveway. If it beads up and rolls off, the existing sealer is still repelling water and doing its job. If it spreads flat and soaks in, the coat is exhausted.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If both tests come back negative — color still deep, water still beading — you don't need to reseal yet, regardless of how many years it's been. Resealing a driveway that doesn't need it wastes money and, more importantly, contributes to the layer buildup that eventually damages the surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-2" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Step 2: Decide Whether You Need to Strip First</h2>
          <p>
            Inspect the existing sealer closely. If it's worn but intact — faded, thin in spots, no longer beading water, but not lifting or cracking — you can clean and coat directly over it. If you see any of the following, strip before recoating:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Flaking or peeling sealer, especially in sheets</li>
            <li>Alligator-pattern cracking in the sealer layer itself (not the underlying pavement)</li>
            <li>Visibly thick, built-up sealer from several past coats applied too frequently</li>
            <li>Sealer that's gone soft, sticky, or tacky in warm weather (a sign of improper past application or moisture entrapment)</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Stripping options range from aggressive pressure washing (2,500+ psi with a rotating surface cleaner attachment) for moderate buildup, to a commercial sealer stripper or mechanical grinding for heavy, multi-layer buildup. Skipping a needed strip and coating over failing sealer just buries the problem for one more season before it fails again, worse.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-3" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Step 3: Clean and Degrease the Surface</h2>
          <p>
            Whether or not you stripped, the surface needs a full clean before recoating. Sweep off debris, scrub any oil or fluid stains with a degreaser (new stains accumulate on every driveway between sealing cycles), pull weeds from cracks and joints, and rinse thoroughly. Let it dry completely — a resealed coat fails from trapped moisture exactly the same way a first-time coat does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-4" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Step 4: Fill Any New Cracks</h2>
          <p>
            Cracks that have appeared since your last sealing need to be filled before you recoat, exactly as they would on a first-time job. It's easy to assume "I already sealed this driveway, it must be fine underneath" — but sealer doesn't stop new cracking from thermal cycling and traffic load between applications. Walk the full surface and fill anything wider than a hairline with a dedicated crack filler before moving on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-5" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Step 5: Apply One Thin, Even Maintenance Coat</h2>
          <p>
            This is the biggest practical difference from first-time sealing: <strong>a maintenance reseal over sound existing sealer typically needs only a single thin coat</strong>, not two. The existing layer is already providing a base level of color and protection — you're topping it up, not building it from zero. Apply with a squeegee-broom applicator (asphalt) or roller (concrete), working in one direction, and resist the temptation to apply it thick "for good measure." Thick, puddled coats are exactly what creates the buildup problem below.
          </p>
          <p>
            Exceptions: if you stripped the old sealer down to bare pavement in Step 2, treat the rest of the job as a first-time application and apply two coats. And if the color test showed significant fading rather than just moderate wear, a second thin coat can restore even color — but check for beading again after the first coat dries before deciding you need a second.
          </p>
          <p>
            Cure time is the same regardless of coat count: keep foot traffic off for at least 24 hours and vehicles off for 48 hours, longer in cool or humid weather.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buildup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">The Buildup Problem: Why "More Often" Isn't Better</h2>
          <p>
            The most common resealing mistake isn't waiting too long — it's resealing too often. Applying a fresh full coat every year, regardless of whether the previous coat is still working, causes sealer to accumulate in layers that get progressively thicker and more brittle with each pass. Eventually that buildup can no longer flex with the pavement's thermal expansion and contraction, and it cracks in the same alligator pattern that damaged asphalt shows — except now it's happening in the sealer layer itself, on top of pavement that might otherwise be fine.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Signs You've Over-Sealed</p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-red-900 text-sm">
              <li>• Sealer layer visibly thick enough to see a "step" at the edge of the driveway</li>
              <li>• Alligator cracking in the sealer that doesn't match cracking in the pavement beneath</li>
              <li>• Sealer peeling away in sheets rather than wearing thin evenly</li>
              <li>• A driveway that's been sealed annually for many consecutive years regardless of condition</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p>
            Once buildup reaches this state, the fix isn't another coat — it's mechanical or chemical stripping of the accumulated layers before any new sealer will bond properly. This is genuinely one of the more labor-intensive driveway repairs and is the direct, avoidable result of resealing on a fixed schedule instead of testing whether it's actually needed. Following Step 1's color and water-bead tests before every reseal is the single best way to avoid ever reaching this point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Resealing Asphalt vs. Resealing Concrete</h2>
        <p>
          Everything above applies most directly to asphalt driveways, since that's where resealing intervals are shortest and the buildup problem is most common. Concrete resealing follows the same underlying logic — test before you coat, clean before you strip — but the specifics differ enough to call out separately.
        </p>
        <p>
          Film-forming acrylic concrete sealers (the "wet look" products) show wear the same way asphalt sealers do: fading gloss and a surface that no longer beads water. Resealing over a worn but intact acrylic coat generally needs one fresh coat, same as asphalt. Where concrete diverges is with <strong>penetrating sealers</strong> — silane/siloxane products that soak into the slab rather than forming a surface film. Because they don't build a visible layer, they don't develop the buildup and cracking problem film-forming products do, and reapplication is more forgiving; a fresh coat every seven to ten years simply re-saturates the pores rather than layering on top of anything. If you're not sure which type is already on your concrete, do a water-bead test in an inconspicuous spot — if the surface stays glossy and beads water, you likely have a film-forming product; if it looks and feels like bare concrete but still beads, you likely have a penetrating sealer.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Tools and Materials for a Reseal Job</h2>
        <p>
          A maintenance reseal needs less gear than a first-time job, but don't shortcut the prep tools. You'll want a stiff push broom and leaf blower for debris, a degreaser and stiff brush for oil spots, and a garden hose or pressure washer for the final rinse. If you're stripping failing sealer, add a pressure washer with a rotating surface-cleaner attachment (or a commercial sealer stripper and a stiff wire brush for smaller problem areas).
        </p>
        <p>
          For the coating itself: a squeegee-broom applicator for asphalt or a 3/8-inch roller for concrete, a crack filler for anything that's opened up since the last sealing, painter's tape to protect edges, and cones or a barrier to keep the driveway closed off during cure time. Buy slightly less sealer than a first-time job would require — a single maintenance coat on a standard two-car driveway typically uses about half what a full two-coat first-time application needs.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Picking the Right Weather Window</h2>
        <p>
          Resealing has the same weather requirements as first-time sealing, and it's tempting to relax them since it feels like a smaller job — resist that. You still need at least 24, ideally 48, hours of dry weather above 50°F with no rain in the forecast and the surface out of harsh direct sun during application. A thin maintenance coat is actually somewhat more vulnerable to a surprise shower than a thick first-time coat, since there's less material buffering against a wash-off. Check the extended forecast, not just the day of, and pick a stretch that gives the coat its full cure window without interruption.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">How Much Sealer to Buy for a Reseal</h2>
        <p>
          Asphalt sealer is sold almost exclusively in 4.75-gallon pails. A single maintenance coat over a sound existing surface typically covers 400–500 square feet per pail — noticeably more than the 300–400 square feet a first coat achieves on porous, unsealed asphalt, since the existing coat is less absorbent. A standard two-car driveway around 600 square feet usually needs just one pail for a maintenance reseal, versus two to three pails for a full first-time two-coat job. Concrete resealing coverage tracks closer to the original application rate, since acrylic and penetrating sealers don't build the same absorption difference asphalt does. Always round up and buy slightly more than the math suggests — a container you don't use keeps for next time, while running short mid-coat leaves a visible seam.
        </p>

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you know when to reseal a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Use two tests: color and water. If the surface has faded from deep black to gray, or water no longer beads and instead soaks in flat, the previous coat has worn out and it's time to reseal. If water still beads, the existing coat is still working — resealing now just adds unnecessary layer thickness.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need to strip old sealer before resealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Not usually. If the old sealer is worn but intact, a thorough clean and degrease is enough before applying a fresh coat over it. Strip only when the existing sealer is actively peeling, alligator-cracked, or has built up excessive thickness from repeated coats.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you reseal a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Every two to three years for standard asphalt emulsion sealers, four to five years for coal-tar or polymer-modified products. Concrete sealers vary from two years (acrylic wet-look) to seven to ten years (penetrating silane/siloxane). Let the water-bead and color tests confirm timing rather than resealing on a fixed calendar.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does resealing take less time than the first sealing job?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Usually. A maintenance reseal over sound existing sealer typically needs only one thin coat instead of two, and prep is limited to cleaning and degreasing. A two-car driveway maintenance reseal can often be done in a single afternoon, versus a full weekend for an initial application with crack repair.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if you reseal a driveway too often?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Sealer builds up in layers that get progressively thicker and more brittle. Eventually the buildup cracks in an alligator pattern and peels away in sheets, requiring mechanical stripping before any new coat can be applied properly. Annual resealing regardless of surface condition is the most common cause.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you reseal a driveway yourself, or should you hire a professional?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Resealing is even more DIY-friendly than a first-time seal job, since it's usually a single thin coat over a surface that's already in decent shape rather than a full restorative application. The material cost for a standard two-car driveway maintenance reseal typically runs well under $100, versus $200–$400 for a professional visit doing the same limited scope of work. Hire a pro mainly if you're also stripping heavy buildup or dealing with extensive new cracking that needs repair first.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I switch sealer brands when I reseal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Generally, no strong reason to switch if the current product has performed well — staying with a similar chemistry (asphalt emulsion over asphalt emulsion, for example) avoids any compatibility questions between coats. If you're upgrading to a polymer-modified or elastomeric product for better durability or crack resistance, that's a reasonable time to switch, since you're already doing a full clean and prep cycle anyway.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it bad to skip a resealing cycle entirely?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Not necessarily. If your driveway still passes the color and water-bead tests when a cycle would typically be due, the existing sealer is still protecting the surface and skipping ahead costs you nothing. Sealing intervals are guidelines based on typical wear, not a hard requirement — the surface's actual condition is always the better indicator than the calendar.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16 border-t border-gray-100 pt-12">
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Related Guides</h3>
