        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Driveway Resealing Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Resealing is different from sealing a bare driveway, because there is already a coating on the surface and its condition decides what happens next.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding a Coat to an Already Thick Build-Up</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer applied annually accumulates into a brittle skin that cracks into a fine network and peels in sheets — damage people frequently mistake for failing asphalt. If the existing build-up is heavy, it needs stripping or a long pause, not another coat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resealing on a Schedule Rather Than on Condition</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The right interval for most residential driveways is two to four years, judged by whether the surface has faded to grey and the aggregate is showing. Sealing a driveway that still looks black is money spent to make the coating more brittle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Removing Loose or Peeling Sealer First</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A new coat bonds to what is underneath it, so applying over flaking sealer just means the new coat leaves with the old one. Scrape and wire-brush the loose areas back to sound material before anything goes down, even though it is the least appealing part of the job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Crack Filling Because the Driveway Was Sealed Before</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cracks open through an existing coat and keep working. Resealing over them bridges the gap for a few weeks and then splits along the same line. Every reseal starts with the same crack inspection and filling as the first one did.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Reseal when the color and water-bead tests say it's time — not on a fixed yearly schedule. Clean over sound existing sealer rather than stripping unnecessarily, apply one thin maintenance coat instead of a full two-coat job, and you'll avoid the buildup that turns a routine maintenance task into an expensive stripping project.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mt-4">
            Treat every reseal as its own small project with its own diagnosis, not an automatic repeat of whatever you did last time. A driveway that needed two coats on its first sealing three years ago might only need one thin maintenance coat this time, and a driveway you assumed was overdue might actually still be protected if water is still beading on the surface. Five minutes with a garden hose before you open a single pail is the cheapest insurance in this entire guide.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mt-4">
            Keep a simple record of when you last sealed and what the surface looked like at the time — a phone photo and a note in your calendar app is enough. That small habit turns every future resealing decision from a guess into a quick, confident check, and it's the single easiest way to make sure your driveway gets exactly the maintenance it needs and nothing more.
          </p>
        </div>
